<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#geji">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geji</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as 歌妓, singer-performer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geji, literally 'singer-performers', were female Chinese performing artists and courtesans trained in singing and dancing. A legendary singer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, Han'e of the Taoist Liezi is held to be the first. Like other entertainers in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> they had low social standing, though some contributed to dance, poetry, painting and literature, and under the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> they sang poems that helped spread them. They were sought chiefly for their artistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and sometimes provided sexual services. Under the Ming their accomplishments were cultivated to set them apart from common prostitutes, and the Qing later banned such courtesans from performing at official functions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geji.jpg" alt="Geji" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Dynasties_Relief_of_Musicians_(11865362735).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
